WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.050
- And a couple of weeks ago

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:02.680
during our singles awareness week,

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.940
I was honored to catch up
with the lovely Al Roker,

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:06.810
I love him so much.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.250
And we both like to talk,

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.360
so, we talked a lot,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.830
and not all of it could
fit into that show.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.760
So today I present to you a Kelly extra,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.640
footage we've never shown before,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.793
here's Al and me nerding
out about being nerds.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.210
Well, like I said earlier
it Singles Awareness Week

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.520
and you've been married
to Deborah for 25 year

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.950
which is amazing.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.133
But what are you like as a single guy?

00:00:31.318 --> 00:00:33.933
- I was pretty pathetic.

00:00:35.026 --> 00:00:35.859
(audience laughs)

00:00:35.859 --> 00:00:40.643
Look, I'm comic book guy (laughs) -

00:00:40.643 --> 00:00:42.620
- That's not pathetic.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.830
- Well, but you don't see
a lot of comic book guys

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:46.663
with dates.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.820
And I know that maybe generalization.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.460
Look, in high school,
I was on the AV squad.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.887
There weren't a lot of
high school girls who said,

00:00:58.887 --> 00:01:00.950
"You know what? I wanna date a guy

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.100
who can thread a projector,"
it just wasn't happening.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:07.510
- My first job was at a movie theater,

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.320
and I always wanted to work projection.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.700
There were only guys up there,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.050
and they got to thread the film,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.810
and I thought it was cool
to be able to splice film

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:15.970
or whatever, so I worked my way in there.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.090
I did, I wasn't supposed
to, sorry district manager,

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.760
but I'm telling you now I did.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.220
And, I went up there and I
got to splice it together

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.600
and I found that really interesting.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.770
But, this is coming from a person that

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.070
plays Scrabble, and reads books,

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.550
and does things (laughs) that
maybe aren't super exciting.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:34.240
- I do the same. Scrabble,
growing up Scrabble

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.330
very big in our household.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:36.163
- Me too.

00:01:36.163 --> 00:01:36.996
- In fact, my mother...

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:39.310
You couldn't make letters
less than three letter,

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.850
you could make words with
less than three letters,

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.066
that was her-

00:01:43.066 --> 00:01:46.030
- My mother, she hates it
when you use QI like chi,

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.997
she gets so mad at my
sister and she's like,

00:01:47.997 --> 00:01:49.760
"Oh if you're gonna cheat,"

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.980
she gets so mad about it (laughs) .

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:52.970
But anyway, I love.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.660
And I actually learned how
to spell with my mother

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.493
using Scrabble.

00:01:56.493 --> 00:01:58.310
She was a huge person into that as well,

00:01:58.310 --> 00:01:59.320
and reading she's an avid reader.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.780
But, anyway I was kinda nerdy
too, but here's my thing,

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.200
there are nerds out there,
so, we just date nerds.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.033
- Yeah.

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:05.950
- We had common interests.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.260
But, tell me about one of
your first dates though

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.700
with Deborah. What was that like?

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.630
- You know, it was funny,

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:15.750
we were in that "friendzone,"
we were friends,

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:20.080
and she said, "Oh, let's
not ruin that friendship."

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.740
And she had gone out,
she was covering an event

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.890
for a couple of weeks,

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.820
and asked me to keep an
eye on her apartment.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.920
So I did, and I had to snoop around.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:31.980
I was in the kitchen,

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.650
and I opened up the fridge,

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:34.880
and there's nothing...

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.609
She's in the background
right now yelling about this.

00:02:37.609 --> 00:02:38.620
(all laugh)

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.140
There's nothing in the fridge,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.260
like some old cheese, a
bottle of Grey Poupon mustard,

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.093
that's about it.

00:02:44.093 --> 00:02:48.390
I open up the oven
expecting to see a mess,

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.260
she still had...

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.720
It was a new oven I guess
when she had moved in,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.090
the cardboard was still
on the oven grates.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.410
I thought, "This woman's
never used this oven,"

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.870
what is this? This is insane.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:04.070
So, a day before she
got back from her trip,

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:07.930
I stocked her pantry, I
stocked her refrigerator,

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.190
and left some flowers on the table,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.630
and a note, "Welcome home."

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.160
And then I got my first
date with her a week later.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.720
- I'm like, "That's a nice entrance sir."

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:18.560
If I come home to my...

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.440
I wasn't far from Deborah by the way.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.100
And by the way you add
some wine to that cheese,

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:23.210
little grape fruit and some crackers,

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.360
it's a nice charcuterie board, whatever.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.852
It's fine, it's a meal for some people Al.

00:03:28.852 --> 00:03:32.600
But that's awesome, that was smooth.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.433
You're smooth.

00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:36.890
You're a nerd, but smooth
nerd, I'm just saying.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.190
So, how do you guys...

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.360
This is a real question,

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.630
how do you guys handle
disagreements as a couple?

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.990
Because, it happens even
when you love each other

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:46.910
you disagree on big and small things.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:47.743
So how have y'all...

00:03:47.743 --> 00:03:50.543
25 years, you're nailing
it, so how are you doing it?

00:03:51.383 --> 00:03:54.900
- Well, the reason I'm nailing
it is because of Deborah.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:59.603
Because, the fact is I pout, I kinda sulk,

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.810
and finally, "What's wrong?"

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.100
I'm like "nothing's wrong, I'm fine,"

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.390
and then she draws it
out of me and we talk.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.760
And eventually we do talk it out,

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.513
but I'm very sensitive.

00:04:18.381 --> 00:04:19.675
Deborah's mom had a saying,

00:04:19.675 --> 00:04:22.177
"Your feelings are as light as a feather."

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.660
And I'm very thin-skinned,
I'm a bit of a baby.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.310
- I guess then what you're saying is

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.900
yin and yang really does
work in a relationship.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:31.733
- Oh yeah.

00:04:31.733 --> 00:04:32.566
- Yeah.

00:04:32.566 --> 00:04:34.150
- It's funny because the kids know,

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.410
like Leila our middle girl will
say, "Mom, give dad a break,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.759
he has so little to live for."

00:04:39.759 --> 00:04:40.592
(all laugh)

00:04:40.592 --> 00:04:43.039
- Oh my God (laughs) !

00:04:43.039 --> 00:04:46.960
(laughing) That's like what just happened?

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.670
I need to have a talk with
her about feelings (laughs) ,

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.450
and the fact that you have them (laughs) .

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:54.710
I literally will look at my...

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:56.270
My kids man, they could be so mean,

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:57.760
and I will look at my
daughter straight in the face

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.130
and I'm like, "Are you
serious? I'm about to cry.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:02.039
That's mean"

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:03.550
(audience applaud)

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.553
- Catch Al Roker every morning
on the "Today" show on NBC.

